# What production's migration ledger says these thirteen files are.
#
# Each line is the md5 and byte length of one row's `statements[1]` in
# `supabase_migrations.schema_migrations` on the live project, read on
# 21 September 2026. The files in this directory were reconstructed from those
# rows and must equal them exactly: `migrationhistory.test.ts` recomputes
# every line and fails on any difference.
#
# These are not checksums of files somebody wrote. They are the fingerprints of
# what the database ran, and the file is only correct because it matches one.
#
# ## The thirteenth, added after the stand-ins landed
#
# `20260921002658_revoke_function_execute_from_supabase_default_roles` was the
# one version with a stand-in in `migrations/` and no record here, and its
# stand-in said why: "it was typed into the dashboard rather than read back
# out". That is not what the ledger says. The row carries one statement, and
#
#     select md5(array_to_string(statements, E'\n'))
#       from supabase_migrations.schema_migrations
#      where version = '20260921002658';
#
# returns `e847a7487970a86c0475047c8ec3ca97`, which is the md5 below and the
# md5 of the file beside it. It was read back out the same way the other twelve
# were — base64 in chunks, each with its own md5, assembled and checked against
# the row before anything was written.
#
# Worth having rather than tidy: that migration is the one that closes the gap
# between `revoke ... from public` and Supabase's `pg_default_acl`, and until
# now the only description of it in this repository was a sentence saying it
# could not be described.
